                <pb facs="0586" n="470" sameAs="frus:frus1914/pg470"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.00/11556.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        Chargé O’Shaughnessy.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1914-04-19T01:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 19, 1914, 1 a.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>760. Your 865, April 18, 8 p.m. The President is out of town. Cannot reach him
                    before eight o’clock tomorrow morning. Am sure <pb facs="0587" n="471"
                        xml:id="pg471"/> however that he would not be willing to have you sign the
                    protocol mentioned. The salute should be fired without any agreement as to the
                    return salute. The United States can be relied upon to do its duty in the matter
                    according to international custom and courtesy. In addition to other reasons
                    signing of protocol would be objectionable because it might be construed as
                    recognition of his Government whereas the President has no intention of
                    recognizing the Huerta Government.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bryan</hi></signed>.</closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
